<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kingdom of Strathclyde</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">5th–11th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a> · Also known as Alt Clut, Kingdom of Alt Clut</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kingdom of Strathclyde was a Brittonic kingdom of the early medieval north, centred first on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dumbarton-rock">Dumbarton Rock</a>, called Alt Clut, and after a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> siege of 870 on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/govan">Govan</a> and the Clyde valley. Its kings fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaels">Gaels</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dal-riata">Dál Riata</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a> and the Northumbrians. In the 11th century it was absorbed into the kingdom of the Scots.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-strathclyde.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Strathclyde" width="320" height="316" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hogweard · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Kingdom_of_Strathclyde_9-10th_century_en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Strathclyde, in Welsh Ystrad Clud, was also known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumbria">Cumbria</a>. It was a Brittonic kingdom of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, part of what Welsh tribes called Yr Hen Ogledd, the Old North, covering parts of what is now southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> and North West England. It emerged in the post-Roman period as the kingdom of Alt Clud, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dumbarton-rock">Dumbarton Rock</a>, and may have been founded by the Damnonii people. Written sources for 400 to 600 are largely Irish and Welsh, and very few are contemporary. Two early kings are known: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceretic-guletic">Coroticus</a>, recipient of a letter from Saint Patrick, and his descendant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhydderch-hael">Rhydderch Hael</a>, named in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adomnan">Adomnán</a>'s Life of Saint <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columba">Columba</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Alt Clut's neighbours were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dal-riata">Dál Riata</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a> and Northumbria, and wars for overlordship were frequent between the 6th and 8th centuries. In 642 the Britons led by Eugein son of Beli defeated Dál Riata at Strathcarron and killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domnall-brecc">Domnall Brecc</a>. In 750 Teudebur of Alt Clut inflicted a heavy defeat on the Picts, and in 756 Pictish and Northumbrian forces besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dumbarton-rock">Dumbarton Rock</a> and obtained the submission of Dumnagual. An army led by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> chiefs <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amlaib-conung">Amlaíb Conung</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imar">Ímar</a> besieged Alt Clut for some four months in 870, destroyed its citadel and took many captives. The capital then seems to have moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/govan">Govan</a>.</p><p>The kingdom expanded south to the Cumbrian Mountains into lands of Rheged, and in the 10th century stretched from Loch Lomond to the River Eamont at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penrith">Penrith</a>. King Owain joined Constantine of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> against the English and was beaten at Brunanburh in 937. In 945 Edmund of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> ravaged Strathclyde. Dyfnwal ab Owain recovered his kingdom and died on pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 975. Owen the Bald is thought to have died at Carham in 1018.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The language of Strathclyde, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumbric">Cumbric</a>, was closely related to Old Welsh. Some time after 1018 and before 1054 the Scots most probably conquered Strathclyde, in the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malcolm-ii-of-scotland">Máel Coluim mac Cináeda</a>, who died in 1034. By the 1070s the Scots again controlled it, and in 1107 Alexander I granted it to his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a>, Prince of the Cumbrians, later David I. Norse or Norse-Gael settlement left place-names and the hogback graves at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/govan">Govan</a>. The area around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlisle">Carlisle</a> was part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> by 1066.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kingdom of Strathclyde” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Domnall Brecc joined Congal Cáech of the Ulaid in his war against the Irish high king and was defeated at the Battle of Mag Rath in 637, which weakened Dál Riata’s position in Ireland; he was killed fighting the <b>Britons of Strathclyde</b> at Strathcarron in 642.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dal-riata">Dal Riata · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>He styled himself king of the English, and in 937 he defeated a coalition of Scots, <b>Strathclyde Britons</b> and Dublin Vikings at the Battle of Brunanburh.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-england">Kingdom of England · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Eadberht (737–758) took Kyle in Ayrshire and in 756, with the Pictish king Óengus, forced the <b>Britons of Dumbarton</b> to submit, but lost most of his army shortly afterwards.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-northumbria">Kingdom of Northumbria · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the early Middle Ages the Picts fought the Gaels of Dál Riata, the <b>Britons of Alt Clut</b> (Dumbarton) and the Angles of Northumbria.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-picts">Kingdom of the Picts · Military</a></li></ul></section>
